Yes—the strongest version of what you’re describing is closed-loop embodied self-regulation: body language is both an output of internal physiology and an input back into it.

The useful distinction is between three layers. At the fast layer, posture, breathing, facial musculature, gaze, movement, imagery, and expectation can measurably alter autonomic state and can influence endocrine signaling indirectly. At the medium layer, repeatedly changing stress, sleep, exercise, feeding, and behavioral state changes the biochemical environment cells experience, which can alter gene expression. At the slow layer, some of those repeated exposures can be associated with epigenetic changes—but we do not currently have evidence that visualization alone lets a person deliberately write specific epigenetic marks on command.

So I’d translate your “astral projecting inward” into a testable mechanism like:

internal simulation → embodied state → autonomic/endocrine shift → altered cellular environment → gene-expression response → possibly longer-term epigenetic adaptation

That preserves the interesting part without making a leap the evidence cannot yet support.

And your delta idea is exactly how you would investigate it rather than merely believe it. Treat the body as both actuator and sensor: generate a precise internal state, embody it, measure what changes, repeat, and learn which imagery/body-state pairings reliably move which outputs. Heart rate, HRV, respiration, skin temperature, pupil size, grip force, reaction time, glucose, and—if you want to go further—salivary cortisol are much easier to measure repeatedly than epigenetics. Epigenetic assays are tissue-specific and comparatively expensive, so they are better treated as a downstream validation layer rather than the immediate feedback channel.

The deeper jackpot is therefore not “thought controls DNA directly.” It is that meaning can become physiology through a cascade of interfaces, and because several of those interfaces are bidirectional, you can learn to steer portions of the cascade deliberately. That is a much larger control surface than most people consciously use.